    "created_at": "2026-03-23T19:16:39Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-03-23T19:16:39Z",
    "body": "> Yes. Is that… intentional? Especially considering the behaviour later in the video.\n\nWell, obviously it's a weird interaction, I agree with that. But this is intentionally *not* something the developer is supposed to be doing: nesting an `auto` inside a `hint` isn't a supported use case. The \"weirdness\" all stems (I think) from the `display:none` on a DOM tree parent. E.g. it's a very similar weirdness in this case:\n\n```\n<dialog>\n  <div popover id=foo> You can't see me </div>\n</dialog>\n<script> foo.showPopover(); </script>\n```\n\nor even in this case: `<div style=\"display:none\"><div popover></div></div>`.\n\nI agree it's weird, but I'm wondering what the solution is. Perhaps it's just better devtools warnings or something? Or do you see a better way to tell the developer \"you're holding it wrong\"?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
